    meaning: "Qingming takes its name from the idea that 'the air is clear, the scenery bright, and all things are revealed'. It falls when the sun reaches celestial longitude 15°, usually between April 4 and 6, and is the fifth solar term of spring. The Lishu (Calendar Book) says: 'At this time all things are clean and orderly, clear and bright, for the season is when the air is clear and the scenery bright and all things are revealed.' It is both a solar term and a traditional festival: Qingming absorbed the customs of the ancient Cold Food Festival and Shangsi Festival, and in the Tang and Song dynasties took shape as a comprehensive grand festival combining tomb-sweeping and ancestor worship with spring outings and merrymaking — unique among the twenty-four solar terms.",
    climate: "By Qingming, apart from the northern Northeast, high-elevation parts of the Northwest and the Tibetan Plateau, daily mean temperatures across most of the country have risen above 12°C and spring sowing and planting swing into high gear. Jiangnan is often overcast and rainy — the weather behind 'rain falls thick and fast at Qingming' — while in South China warm and cold air masses tug back and forth and severe convective weather (thunderstorms, gales, hail) increases markedly. North China is dry with little rain and frequent strong winds, and forest fire risk is high, hence the twin watchwords 'around Qingming, plant melons and sow beans' and guard against fire. Late-spring cold snaps can still strike Jiangnan, threatening early-rice nurseries and fruit-tree bloom.",
